course: dictionary
lesson: 6568
title: Words for people and places
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A word can describe a thing, like «Nigerian food», or name a person, like a Nigerian. The first kind is an adjective: it tells you more about a noun. The second is a noun: it names someone. Both come from Nigeria, a large country in West Africa.
explain: Look at the words around the word. «Nigerian music» tells you the music comes from Nigeria. «Nigerian English» tells you the English is spoken the Nigerian way. When you see Nigerian, ask: what is this thing or person connected with Nigeria?
explain: Capital letters matter. Nigerian always starts with a capital N because it names a country and its people. Nigeria, the country, is a place name, so it has a capital letter too.
fact: Nigeria is a large country in West Africa.
fact: Nigeria has the largest population in Africa, and many languages are spoken there, such as Yoruba, Igbo and Hausa.
fact: English is the official language of Nigeria, so «Nigerian English» is English as Nigerians speak it.
fact: «Jollof rice» is a Nigerian food that is popular all over the world.
fact: A Nigerian is a person from Nigeria, and «Nigerians» means more than one person.
exercise: In «Nigerian music», what does Nigerian describe? | The place where the music is played | *The music, which comes from Nigeria | The singer's age
exercise: «Ada is a Nigerian.» What kind of word is Nigerian here? | A verb, because it shows an action | *A noun, because it names a person | An adjective, because it describes a thing
exercise: Why does «Nigerians» start with a capital letter? | It doesn't matter, capitals are optional | Because it is a verb | *Because it names people from a country | Because it is always the first word of a sentence
test: In «In Nigerian English "I'm coming" can mean "I'll be back soon"», what does Nigerian tell you? | It names a city in Africa | *It describes the English, which is spoken the Nigerian way | It is a verb meaning to come back
test: Which of these is a Nigerian food? | *Jollof rice | Pizza | Fish and chips | Shepherd's pie
exam: Which sentence uses Nigerian correctly? | *She cooked Nigerian jollof rice for the class | The sky is a bright Nigerian colour | Nigerian runs very fast down the hill | She is Nigerian in the morning
